# Queue writes need idempotency keys

## Context

A blind retry of a queue write duplicates the message when the first attempt actually landed. Without idempotency keys, retrying failed sends or implementing automatic retry logic risks processing the same message multiple times.

## Guidance

Attach an idempotency key to every message before the first send, and retry only with the same key. The key should be:

- Unique per logical operation (not per attempt)
- Stable across retries
- Included in the message body or metadata

Example pattern:

```typescript
// ❌ Without idempotency key (duplicates on retry)
for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {
  try {
    await queue.send({ body });
    break;
  } catch (error) {
    // Retry will duplicate the message if first attempt succeeded
  }
}

// ✅ With idempotency key (safe to retry)
const idempotencyKey = crypto.randomUUID();
for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {
  try {
    await queue.send({
      body,
      idempotencyKey
    });
    break;
  } catch (error) {
    // Same key ensures retry doesn't duplicate
  }
}
```

## Why This Matters

Without idempotency keys, you risk:
- Duplicate message processing
- Data corruption (double-charging, duplicate records)
- Race conditions where first send succeeds but retry still happens

Queue systems cannot reliably distinguish "retry of failed send" from "intentional duplicate send" without an idempotency key.

## When to Apply

- Every queue write operation (src/queue.ts:`publish` method)
- Any code path that retries queue sends
- Background job queue operations

## Examples

Queue consumer should detect and skip duplicate messages using the idempotency key:

```typescript
// On the consumer side
const processedKeys = new Set();

async function handleMessage(message) {
  if (processedKeys.has(message.idempotencyKey)) {
    return; // Already processed, skip
  }

  // Process the message
  await doWork(message);
  processedKeys.add(message.idempotencyKey);
}
```

